  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe remnants of our nor'easter and associated parent low aloft\nwill continue to slowly decay over New England today with \nplenty of low and mid-level moisture lingering overhead. While a\npositively tilted ridge will build eastward into western NY/PA \ntoday, its ridge axis will remain displaced well to our west \nmost of the day. Therefore, expecting yet another dreary day\nunder mostly cloudy skies with a few isolated showers still \naround (including drizzle/mist this morning); however, we will\nnot experience widespread steady showers as we did the past few\ndays. The ridge finally builds east enough this evening to \nsupport partial clearing skies. However, with plenty of low- \nlevel moisture still in place, any clearing can easily support \nareas of fog tonight, especially in valley areas. Then, our \nupper level ridge and sfc high take control of the Northeast \nWednesday into Thursday supporting a stretch of dry weather with\ntemperatures trending warmer than normal thanks to persist \nsouthwest flow aloft advecting a warmer air mass into the \nregion. In fact, there is at least a 70% chance that high \ntemperatures on Wednesday and Thursday exceed 75 degrees in \nvalley areas. While temperatures aloft will be quite warm, \nmoisture streaming up and over a closed high parked over the \nSoutheast U.S should support at least partly cloudy skies, \nlimiting temperatures from becoming unseasonably warm. NBM \nprobabilities show only a few isolated areas with less than a \n20% chance for highs either day to exceed 80 degrees. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure slides off the East Coast by Thursday with a split\nflow pattern setting up as a ridge of high pressure remains\nparked over the Southeast U.S while another shortwave within \nthe northern branch of the jet stream slides eastward through \nthe northern Great Lakes. A cold front ahead of the trough will \nprogress eastward across New York into western New England on \nFriday. Southwest flow around the periphery of ridge centered to\nour south looks to support a moisture fetch out of the eastern \nPacific, funneling plenty of moisture north and eastward from \nthe Desert Southwest into the Great Lakes/Northeast. Latest \nNAEFS show PWATs within this moisture plume ranging 2 to 3 \nstandard deviations above normal and becoming focused ahead of \nour incoming cold front. While there is increasing confidence \nthat this set-up will likely support a period of rain (steady at\ntimes) as the front tracks northwest to southeast during the \nday Friday (favoring Fri afternoon), fast flow aloft will allow \nsteady rain to be rather progressive. In fact, NBM probabilistic\nguidance shows less than a 20% chance for rainfall amounts to \nexceed 0.50\" regionwide. Therefore, flooding issues are not \nanticipated. Otherwise, Friday will remain warmer than normal \nbefore a strong area of high pressure quickly rebuilds over the \nNortheast for Saturday, trending temperatures down closer to \nnormal and giving us a dry start to the weekend.",
